  "context_pill": "Capital Gains on Inherited Property",
  "example_summary": "Abraham inherited a house with a mortgage. To get a clear title, he paid off the loan. We calculate his capital gains by combining the original cost and the mortgage payment.",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Summary Matrix\n\n| Statutory Provision | Core Condition & Trigger | Legal Consequence & Tax Treatment |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Section 49(1)(iiia)** | Property acquired under a Will or inheritance | Cost of acquisition shall be deemed to be the cost for which the previous owner acquired the property. |\n| **Section 48 r.w. Section 55(2)** | Payment of outstanding mortgage loan by successor | Expenditure incurred for clearing mortgage debt to obtain a clear title qualifies as cost of acquisition. |\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Judicial Precedent (RM. Arunachalam v. CIT):** Payment of an outstanding mortgage debt by the legal heir to secure a release of mortgage and obtain a clear title is considered part of the cost of acquisition under Section 48.\n\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Holding Period Determination:** For inherited capital assets, the period of holding includes the period held by the previous owner, determining whether the resulting capital gain is Long-Term Capital Gain (LTCG).",
